  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The Angels buried them early. By the end of the fourth inning, Los Angeles had scored nine runs and the game was functionally over. Rodr\u00edguez lasted 3.2 innings, and the bullpen couldn't slow the damage. David Bednar gave up three more in the eighth. The Yankees generated six hits and ten strikeouts. They never led once.",
    "what_this_means": "Fragility has a shape, and this was it. They found nothing early when the game was still there. One chance in the first inning, runners in scoring position, and they didn't convert. That was the only window. By the time they woke up, they were chasing nine runs in a game that had already been decided by the fifth inning. Ten strikeouts isn't a reflection of bad luck or cold swings. It's a team pressing against an outcome that was no longer recoverable. The pitching gave them nothing to work with, but the offense wasn't ready when it mattered. Two runners on base and nothing to show for it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Grayson Rodriguez carries a 6.03 ERA into tonight, but Cole's 2.52 mark over his last four starts has to hold up if the Yankees want to avoid getting buried again.",
